 ## Is *Sasa* expansion a form of shrubification? {#sec-disc-shrub}
 
-Woody shrub expansion is among the best-documented vegetation responses to warming in Arctic and alpine tundra [@MyersSmith2011ERL; @Bjorkman2018Nature; @Carlson2017ERL]. Although *Sasa* is not a woody shrub, it behaves functionally like expanding tundra shrubs: it forms dense, tall, evergreen stands that exclude subordinate species [@Kudo2011EcoEvo], and snowbed vegetation recovers after its removal [@Kudo2017AlpBot]. It differs from typical shrubs in two respects, however. First, rhizome expansion proceeds from existing fronts with a metre-scale dispersal kernel, making it more spatially predictable than the spread of shrubs that establish new nuclei through seed dispersal and, in principle, amenable to targeted removal at the front. Second, *Sasa* competes with dwarf pine, which is also expanding under warming [@Amagai2015EcoRes]. The bidirectional *Sasa*–dwarf pine exchanges in the transition matrix suggest that these two expanding taxa are partly replacing each other as well as snowbed vegetation. This interaction merits study in its own right rather than being treated solely as an observational complication.
