 ## Ground-based cameras among vegetation monitoring approaches {#sec-disc-method}
 
-The role of ground-based cameras in this framework is complementary to that of satellites. Satellite time series offer continuity over decades and comparability among regions, making them well suited to analyses of landscape-scale greening and range shifts. Ground-based cameras provide sub-metre resolution and daily revisits within a fixed field of view, matching the scale of processes underlying the changes observed by satellites: patch-front advance, the sequence of snowmelt within snowbeds, and community boundaries. Our study illustrates what becomes possible in this gap: an establishment model fitted at the resolution of the process. The limitations are also specific: a fixed oblique viewpoint, coverage of at most a few km², dependence on continuous imagery throughout the period of interest, and restriction to taxa distinguishable in photographs, such as large or phenologically distinctive plants like *Sasa* and dwarf pine. The last limitation could be partly relaxed through field collection of training data.
