 ## Study site {#sec-study-site}
 
-The study slope lies on the western side of Mt. Tateyama (Murodo-daira and the surrounding slopes, 2,350–3,015 m a.s.l.) in the northern Japanese Alps, within Chubu-Sangaku National Park, Toyama Prefecture. The region is among the snowiest alpine environments in the world. Airborne laser surveys at peak snow accumulation show that mean snow depth on the western slope of Mt. Tateyama increases linearly with elevation, reaching approximately 7 m at Murodo-daira, with substantially greater local depths in snowdrifts [@Iida2006JSSI]. The plateau consists of pyroclastic deposits from Tateyama Volcano, and the slopes are dissected by avalanche tracks and hollows that retain snow. Snowmelt progresses from wind-exposed ridges, typically in May–June, to snowbed hollows where snow persists into August, creating steep gradients in growing-season length over horizontal distances of tens of metres.
+The study slope lies on the western side of Mt. Tateyama (Murodo-daira and the surrounding slopes, 2,350–3,015 m a.s.l.) in the northern Japanese Alps, within Chubu-Sangaku National Park, Toyama Prefecture. The region is among the snowiest alpine environments in the world. Airborne laser surveys at peak snow accumulation show that mean snow depth on the western slope of Mt. Tateyama increases linearly with elevation, reaching approximately 6–7 m at Murodo-daira, with substantially greater local depths in snowdrifts [@Iida2006JSSI]. The plateau consists of pyroclastic deposits from Tateyama Volcano, and the slopes are dissected by avalanche tracks and hollows that retain snow. Snowmelt progresses from wind-exposed ridges, typically in May–June, to snowbed hollows where snow persists into August, creating steep gradients in growing-season length over horizontal distances of tens of metres.
 
 The vegetation forms a mosaic typical of the snowy alpine zone of central Japan: dwarf pine scrub on exposed sites, snowbed communities in late-melting hollows, tall-forb meadows on moist slopes, and deciduous shrubs (rowans, maple, and montane alder) along streams and avalanche tracks. *Sasa* forms dense stands near the lower limit of the alpine zone and occurs as scattered patches within the study slope. Although the area receives intensive tourist use via the Tateyama Kurobe Alpine Route, visitors remain on maintained trails, and the observed slope is not directly affected by trampling. No vegetation management or *Sasa* removal took place on the slope during the study period.
 
 ## Image acquisition {#sec-image-acquisition}
 
-We used images captured by a digital time-lapse camera (EOS 5D Mark II, Canon Inc., 21 megapixels) installed by the National Institute for Environmental Studies on the Murodo-sanso mountain lodge (approximately 2,450 m a.s.l.). Since 2010, the camera has photographed the slope hourly from 6:00 to 19:00 between April and November. For details of the installation and operation, see @Okamoto2024RSEC. Vegetation classification used images acquired in August–October of 2012 and 2021 (7 frames per year), and snowmelt detection used images from April–August of each year during 2011–2021. Data for 2019 are missing because of construction work at the mountain lodge housing the camera. Images from 2010 were excluded because a different lens gave an incompatible field of view. Snowmelt detection followed the workflow of @IdeOguma2013EcolInfom based on Otsu's binarisation method [@otsu_binarization]. Detectable snowmelt dates were censored by the imaging season (approximately DOY 120–230), and pixels without valid detections, including sky and permanently occluded terrain, were treated as missing.
