 ## Quantification of *Sasa* change {#sec-sasa-change}
 
-We overlaid the 2012 and 2021 vegetation maps (Figure S6) and calculated a transition matrix for all classes. Throughout, we distinguish three quantities: **gross expansion**, the area transitioning from non-*Sasa* to *Sasa*; **gross loss**, the area transitioning from *Sasa* to non-*Sasa*; and **net change**, their difference. Areas are reported as the sum of the actual areas of 1 m cells measured on the Earth's ellipsoid (geodesic area). These are nearly identical to nominal areas calculated as cell count × 1 m² (difference <0.1%); figure legends and model sample sizes refer to cell counts. Because *Sasa* can persist beneath shrub canopies, apparent loss may reflect canopy cover or shifts in the classification of mixed *Sasa*–shrub pixels rather than true decline. We report the destination classes of loss in the Results and address this ambiguity in the Discussion. To assess this interpretation empirically, we conducted a blind interpretation of paired images. A total of 129 samples comprised 79 stratified samples of loss cells, proportional to the composition of destination classes, 40 stable controls (20 *Sasa* in both years and 20 dwarf pine in both years), and 10 reverse transitions (dwarf pine→*Sasa*). Paired image crops from 2012 and 2021 were presented in random order with transition types concealed. Each sample included two pairs, one from the green-leaf season and one from the autumn-colour season; frames with similar image greenness were paired to reduce misinterpretation arising from differences in leaf colour between acquisition dates. The first author assigned each sample to one of four interpretation categories: *Sasa* loss, canopy cover, boundary ambiguity, or indeterminate (full design and results in the Supplementary Information).
