+## Terrain predictors and Table S11: predictor correlation matrix and VIF
 
-Terrain predictors (slope, aspect, TPI, and TWI) were computed from the Fundamental Geospatial Data DEM of the Geospatial Information Authority of Japan (approximately 5 m mesh) [@GSIdem] in QGIS using SAGA GIS tools, and then reprojected to the 1 m analysis grid by bilinear interpolation. The effective resolution of the terrain predictors is therefore that of the source DEM rather than 1 m.
+Elevation was taken from the Fundamental Geospatial Data DEM of the Geospatial Information Authority of Japan [@GSIdem], whose native 0.2″ × 0.2″ grid corresponds to cells of 4.97 m (east–west) × 6.16 m (north–south) after projection to JGD2000 / UTM zone 53N (EPSG:3099). Slope, aspect, and TPI were derived from this DEM with the GDAL `gdaldem` utility: slope and aspect with the Zevenbergen–Thorne four-neighbour algorithm [@Zevenbergen1987ESPL], and TPI as the difference between the elevation of the focal cell and the mean elevation of its eight neighbours (a fixed 3 × 3 window). We verified this provenance post hoc by re-deriving each layer from the archived DEM with `gdaldem` (GDAL 3.12), which reproduced the archived rasters exactly (maximum absolute difference 0). TWI [@Beven1979TWI] was computed in QGIS with SAGA GIS [@Conrad2015SAGA] at the tool's default settings; the specific module, its parameters, and the QGIS and SAGA version numbers were not recorded at the time of processing and could not be recovered, although the archived layer is consistent with a multiple-flow-direction contributing area combined with a lower bound on slope. Aspect was decomposed into northness and eastness at the native DEM resolution, and all terrain layers were then reprojected onto the 1 m analysis grid by bilinear interpolation; their effective resolution therefore remains that of the source DEM rather than 1 m. The derived terrain rasters are included in the archived deposit. The full predictor correlation structure is given in Table S11.
 
